A||||15||madunham||Polyurethane bushings - what's the story?
Z||000000||madunham||05-05-99||06:59 AM||||I posted the question on "Ultimate" dog bones, and got another favorable review for the poly bushings, but I still wonder ... what do they REALLY do?  For example, what does poly do in the dog bone besides hold the engine more firmly?  How exactly does that transfer to the ground, or does it?  Secondly, if they are as wonderful as their reputation seems to be, what is a guy going to get into if he wanted to replace his suspension & sway arm bushings?  Is that best left to your trusty mechanic who has the RIGHT equipment?  If so, does anyone know what it costs (besides the cost of the poly parts)?
Z||000001||theogre||05-05-99||07:51 AM||||urethane doesn't compress like rubber. This keeps the alignment more acurate under a wider range of conditions.<p>installing urethane generally isn't dificult but time consuming. Depending on just what source of bushings you use, you may need access to a press.<p>If you are doing suspension, you should do the entire suspension at once. at the least you should do the whole front control arm set or the whole back arm set in one shot of you have to spread out the time. you can do the sway bar, motor moounts, and cradel, as a seperate sections of a complete project if you need.<p>The car will also need alignment after control arms and cradel are done.
Z||000002||PaUL||05-05-99||02:00 PM||||for poly control arm bushings, try this site, they got the lowest prices I could find:  <A HREF="http://www.suspension.com/fiero-prothane.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.suspension.com/fiero-prothane.htm</A> 
Z||000003||RICK||05-05-99||05:14 PM||||I just put all poly bushing on the front of my fiero.  Time consuming is right!  <p>I had a lot of problems getting the 12 year old ORGINAL shocks off (I just bought it, not my fault)and other bolts out.  I did need a press to get the orginal rubber bushing out, once they were out the poly bushings pressed right in with no problem.<br>The only problem I'm having now is they sweek when I hit a bump.  I have to assume that the sweek will go away when the bushing wear in some.
Z||000004||Carrolles||05-06-99||08:09 AM||||I've heard that Poly bushings must be lubricated with a Lithium grease when installed or else they will always squeak.
Z||000005||Patrick||05-06-99||12:53 PM||||<br>I thought I was the only one who still had the original shocks on my Fiero.:)<p>I would imagine that to get the lithium grease properly applied to the poly bushings that everything would have to be disassembled again.<p>Do you think that Rick will be upset when he reads these last two posts?<p> 
Z||000006||theogre||05-06-99||02:22 PM||||There are notes on that web site above about greasing, why, and some methods for how.<p>There are some bushings with graphite in them,,, it's suposed to help kill sqwalking but I've never gotten to use them yet.
Z||000007||PaUL||05-06-99||02:29 PM||||hey Rick,<br>how did your car handle after you installed the poly bushing? Is it worth the money and work to install these?
Z||000008||PaUL||05-06-99||02:31 PM||||Ive also read that you can drill and tap a hole for a grease fitting on some poly bushings. 
Z||000009||RICK||05-06-99||05:03 PM||||Paul, night and day difference.  It is very, very tight up front now.  I replaced all bushing up front with poly, all ball joints are new, tie rod ends, and steering stabalizer, and shocks.  I did use the greese that they sent with the bushings.  The directions said to apply it to help get the bushings into the sleeve.  I think the sweeking is comming from the edge of the bushing rubbing aganist the mounts. (I hope)
Z||000010||PaUL||05-07-99||12:54 AM||||I just changed all the front balljoints a few weeks ago. I should have done the bushings at the same time. Did you press all the old bushings off or did you have to burn some out with a torch? Did you use the ones from Prothane? Did they come without a metal shell?
Z||000011||RICK||05-10-99||07:04 AM||||Paul, At first we used a torch but that was a mess, we took the arms to my friends shop and we used a 20 ton press to pop them out.  You have to be careful when your doing this or you can bend the arm.  the metal stays in the arm and you just punch out the rubber.  The poly bushing will press right back into place because they do not come with a metal cover.  I got mine from The Fiero Store and they are the prothane.
Z||000012||theogre||05-12-99||02:50 PM||||prothane has a web site, and there is a large vendor of thiers on line....<br> <A HREF="http://www.prothane.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.prothane.com</A> <br> <A HREF="http://www.suspension.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.suspension.com</A> <p>both cover a bunch of stuff in the way of tech tips.<p>On posibility to eliminating squeeks caused by the flat faces of poly bushings rubbing the frame would be to install some thin washers. I imagine thin brass sheet, or LDPE plastic (commonly used for takeout soup container lids), would work well in this application. That would give you a smooth surface that wouldn't corode up on you and grab onto the bushings. It would also slide better on the rough frame.<p>you would still have to properly install the bushings with the right grease, but in areas like the face where grease washes out easy this might be a good long term fix. BTW Prothane has a grease they have specifically tested for this use..... from it's description it sounds allot like disk brake caliper pin grease.<p>
Z||000013||Racer 1||06-01-99||10:20 AM||||I put them blasted "sqeeking pig" sounding bushing's in my fiero.The car handles better but the ride over rough roads is bad - call 911 my back just went out kinda ride.Enless you race stay away from these bushings and use the stock rubber ones!
Z||000014||GREENDANO||06-06-99||03:04 PM||||I have urethane bushing in my GT.  I have yet to hear a noise from any of them.  When I put my motor and subframe back in I didn't put my rear sway bar back in (future mods to it on the way)  The car handles better without the bar and w/urethane as it did with the bar and stock bushings.  As far as ride quality, I have Monroe gas shocks at all four corners, it is firm, but isn't that kind-of what a "sports car" is??<br>  Dan-O
Z||000015||lowCG||06-07-99||02:19 PM||||With not as much weight on the front end,and no subframe,the rubber bushings work pretty good.Was going to try some urethane ones,on the bottom only,but can't justify the loss in ride quality for only a marginal gain in handling.I've tried to change only one thing at a time to be sure of the effect,takes longer,but you know what you have when you're done.Has anyone tried the front urethane treatment after doing the rear?<br>The two ends of the car have very different requirements in many ways,yet still affect one another at the same time. 
